  <h1>Dengue NS1 vs IgM/IgG Rapid Tests</h1>
  <p>Two different targets, two different phases of infection — and a combined device that covers both.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>NS1 Antigen</th><th>IgM/IgG Antibody</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>What it detects</th><td>Viral NS1 protein (active virus)</td><td>Immune response to infection</td></tr>
        <tr><th>First detectable</th><td>Day 1 of fever</td><td>IgM ~day 3–5; IgG later</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Best window</th><td>First week (early acute)</td><td>Late acute / convalescent</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What it tells you</th><td>Active infection</td><td>Exposure / past or secondary infection</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Key caveat</th><td>Serotype-dependent sensitivity</td><td>Flavivirus cross-reactivity (Zika)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to choose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Early presentation (≤7 days):</strong> NS1 antigen is the better first test.</li>
    <li><strong>Later / convalescent:</strong> IgM/IgG serology adds value.</li>
    <li><strong>Unknown timing:</strong> a combined NS1 + IgM/IgG device covers the widest window in one test.</li>
  </ul>

